const e={type:"config",goal:"id4d",title:"Why Legal Identification Matters For Global Development",shortTitle:"ID4D",abstract:"Over the past decade, the world has made steady progress toward universal ID coverage. Yet hundreds of millions of people still have no way to prove who they are. Most of these are children and the poor, and the gap is widest in Sub-Saharan Africa. What will it take to close the global ID gap?",citation:'Pirlea, A.F., Wolff, J.M., and Lambrechts, M. 2026. “<a href="https://doi.org/10.60616/q660-3q95" target="_blank" rel="external">Why Legal Identification Matters For Global Development</a>”  In Atlas of Global Development 2026, edited by A F Pirlea, D Wadhwa, D Mahler, U Serajuddin, A Thudt, and M Welch. Washington DC: World Bank. License: Creative Commons Attribution CC BY 3.0 IGO.',credits:"Ana Florina Pirlea & Jorin Margaux Wolff",inputs:"Saniya Ansar, Lorena Corso, Julia Michal Clark",visuals:"Maarten Lambrechts,  Illustrations: Drew Bardana",homeFactoidNumber:"1 in 10",homeFactoidPercent:"10",homeFactoidText:"people worldwide lack an official ID",homeTagline:"Hundreds of millions of people still lack legal proof of identity. Most of them are children, the poor, and other vulnerable groups, primarily in Sub-Saharan Africa.",theme:"digital",publicationDate:"2026-12-01",slug:"why-legal-identification-matters-for-global-development",keyFacts:[{_key:"784a0b6f9b32",contentBlockKey:"fb2a5b468e9a",keyFact:"805 million",keyFactLabel:"people still lack proof of legal identity."},{_key:"a8b4971be1d6",contentBlockKey:"d975155f0507",keyFact:"2.8 billion",keyFactLabel:"people live in economies without access to digital IDs. "},{_key:"bbf8a48086ab",contentBlockKey:"58faf33f40fc",keyFact:"2–3 times",keyFactLabel:"higher — the rate at which adults with digital IDs access online government services, compared with those without."}]},a=[{_key:"k10298-0aa9fc0e7ff5",type:"text",text:`An official form of ID provides access to social programs, health care, education, and legal protections. It also allows people to open a bank account or get a SIM card, work in the formal sector, run a business, and own land and other assets. Yet, millions of people, particularly those in lower-income economies and marginalized or vulnerable groups, still lack an official government-issued ID that confirms their legal identity.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_1_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_1_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"59d1ffbbe949",type:"subhed",text:"Who lacks official proof of identity?"},{_key:"f1b3227461a0-7fa04d4b2e5b",type:"text",text:"Over the past decade, the world has made steady progress on this front. Between 2017 and 2025, the number of people without proof of legal identity fell from around 1 billion to 805 million, even as the world’s population grew. Coverage is now 95 percent among adults, with the remaining gap increasingly concentrated among children. More than half of those without ID are children whose births were never registered."},{_key:"fb2a5b468e9a",type:"vis",graphic:"no_id_global_trend",title:"In 2025, over 800 million people lacked proof of legal identity, down from 1 billion in 2017",subtitle:"",source:'<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a>',visSize:"default",visdescription:"A stacked bar chart shows the number of people without legal identification, in millions, for three periods: 2017, 2021–2022 and 2025. Each bar has two parts: adults without foundational ID (orange, bottom) and children without birth registration (blue, top). In 2017 the total was 1,000 million, made up of 530 million adults and 470 million children. In 2021–2022 it fell to 843 million: 417 million adults and 426 million children. In 2025 it was 805 million: 347 million adults and 458 million children. The number of adults without ID fell steadily over the period. The number of unregistered children dipped and then rose again, so by 2025 children made up more than half of the people without legal identity.",data:"no_id_global_trend",anchorKey:"fb2a5b468e9a",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,adults_without_id:"Adults without foundational ID",children_without_registration:"Children without birth registration",y_axis_title:"People without legal identification",y_axis_units:"millions",keyFact:"805 million",keyFactLabel:"people still lack proof of legal identity."},{_key:"97898db04e44-147bf522319a",type:"text",text:`The global identification gap is also heavily concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa, which accounts for more than half of the world’s unregistered population. The number of children in this region without birth registration exceeds the number of adults without foundational ID, showing how exclusion can begin at birth and persist later in life. The balance flips in regions like East Asia and Pacific or Latin America and the Caribbean, where adults account for the larger share of the identification gap.<div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1364758319_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1364758319_1'/><div class="details">The cutoff age for splitting populations into children and adults is determined by the adult metric used when collecting the data. For Findex data, the cutoff is either the minimum legal age for obtaining the foundational ID or age 15, whichever is higher. For voter registration data, the cutoff is the voting age, typically 18. For administrative data, the cutoff is either ID eligibility age, or age 5 in economies that allow registration from birth. The under-5 birth registration rate was then applied to the entire child population below the cutoff age. There are no cross-nationally comparable registration data for the 5–15 age range, so the under-5 rate serves as a proxy for all children below ID age. The rate is an under-5 rate, but the population it is applied to is roughly 0–14 in most economies.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 2"><label for='reference_2_1_1129214132' aria-hidden="true">2</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_2_1_1129214132'/><div class="details">Clark, J.M., Metz, A.Z., & Casher, C.S. (2022). <a href="http://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099705012232226786" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Database 2021: Global ID Coverage Estimates.</a> Washington, DC: World Bank.</div></div></div></div><div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1999820947_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1999820947_1'/><div class="details">Reported gaps in ID ownership may also include people whose credentials have been lost or expired, and those who do not perceive a need to own an ID.</div></div>`},{_key:"d3e5e22d980b",type:"vis",graphic:"no_id_regions",title:"Sub-Saharan Africa has the highest number of both unregistered children and adults without ID",subtitle:"",source:'<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"Two side-by-side horizontal bar charts compare seven world regions. The left chart (blue) shows children without birth registration, in millions. The right chart (orange) shows adults without ID, in millions. Sub-Saharan Africa leads both by a wide margin, with 263 million unregistered children and 156 million adults without ID. Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan and Pakistan follows with 86 million children and 37 million adults. South Asia has 70 million children and 26 million adults. East Asia and Pacific has 29 million children but 60 million adults. Latin America and the Caribbean has 9 million children and 12 million adults. Europe and Central Asia has 1 million children and 26 million adults. North America has close to zero children and 31 million adults. In Sub-Saharan Africa, Middle East, North Africa, Afghanistan and Pakistan, and South Asia, unregistered children outnumber adults without ID. In the other four regions, adults make up the larger share of the gap.",data:"no_id_regions",anchorKey:"d3e5e22d980b",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,children:"Children without birth registration",adults:"Adults without ID"},{_key:"1cd9a3f65e61",type:"subhed",text:"Children without birth registration"},{_key:"8e0039c73c50-61c416536ea9",type:"text",text:`Birth registration is usually a child’s first recognition in the eyes of the state, providing the legal identity needed to access rights and opportunities throughout life.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_1_169125019' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_1_169125019'/><div class="details">UNICEF. (2024). <a href="https://data.unicef.org/resources/the-right-start-in-life-2024-update/" target="_blank" rel="external">The right start in life: Global levels and trends in birth registration 2024 update</a>. New York, NY: United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF).</div></div> Without it, children can be excluded from education, health care, and social protection, increasing their vulnerability to poverty and social exclusion.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_2_169125019' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_2_169125019'/><div class="details">UNICEF. (2024). <a href="https://data.unicef.org/resources/the-right-start-in-life-2024-update/" target="_blank" rel="external">The right start in life: Global levels and trends in birth registration 2024 update</a>. New York, NY: United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF).</div></div><div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 4"><label for='reference_4_1_885055261' aria-hidden="true">4</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_4_1_885055261'/><div class="details">Kusumaputra, I. M., Mahardika, M. C., Hayati, B. N., & Itu, Y. (2025). <a href="https://hal.science/hal-05113370v1/document" target="_blank" rel="external">Exclusion from birth: Structural barriers and social impacts absence of birth certificates for children of urban vulnerable groups</a>. The Journal of Philosophical Economics: Reflections on Economic and Social Issues (in press). HAL Archive. </div></div> Lack of birth registration also heightens the risk of child labor, trafficking, child marriage, and other forms of exploitation, as well as statelessness, particularly among marginalized, migrant, and refugee populations.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 5"><label for='reference_5_1_1924246188' aria-hidden="true">5</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_5_1_1924246188'/><div class="details">European Network on Statelessness (ENS). (2024). <a href="https://www.statelessness.eu/sites/default/files/2024-11/ENS_Birth_Registration_briefing_Nov2024_FINALDESIGN_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Birth registration and the prevention of statelessness in Europe: Identifying good practices and remaining barriers (Thematic briefing, updated November 2024)</a>. European Network on Statelessness.</div></div><div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_3_169125019' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_3_169125019'/><div class="details">UNICEF. (2024). <a href="https://data.unicef.org/resources/the-right-start-in-life-2024-update/" target="_blank" rel="external">The right start in life: Global levels and trends in birth registration 2024 update</a>. New York, NY: United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF).</div></div>  The lack of birth registration can also make it much more difficult for people to obtain a formal, government-issued ID as adults.`},{_key:"4a6e8052f5d7-3d207c276b8b",type:"text_emphasis",text:`For children under the age of ID eligibility, proof of legal identity is provided through birth registration.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_2_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_2_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"3f1e6fdb7ca6-61c416536ea9",type:"text",text:`The under-5 birth registration rate is the only internationally comparable measure of birth registration. This is near universal across Europe, North America, and much of Latin America, East Asia & the Pacific, but falls sharply in South Asia (76 percent) and Sub-Saharan Africa, where only 52 percent of young children are registered. In a handful of countries, including Ethiopia, Somalia and Zambia, fewer than 1 child in 10 are registered.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_4_169125019' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_4_169125019'/><div class="details">UNICEF. (2024). <a href="https://data.unicef.org/resources/the-right-start-in-life-2024-update/" target="_blank" rel="external">The right start in life: Global levels and trends in birth registration 2024 update</a>. New York, NY: United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF).</div></div><div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_908030682_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_908030682_1'/><div class="details">Birth registration records a child’s existence in the civil registry, while a birth certificate provides documentary proof of that registration. It is therefore possible for a child to be registered but lack a birth certificate.</div></div> `},{_key:"5786c78bbbf8",type:"vis",graphic:"birth_registration_choropleth",title:"Birth registration rates are lowest in Sub-Saharan Africa",subtitle:"Birth registration rates (% of children under 5)",source:'UNICEF. 2026. <a href="https://data.unicef.org/topic/child-protection/birth-registration/" target="_blank" rel="external">Birth registration data</a> (latest available year, data as of April 2026, accessed October 2026)',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"A world map shows the share of children under 5 whose births are registered. Colors run from pale cream (0 percent) through light blue to dark navy (100 percent). Most of the Americas, Europe, North Africa, Central Asia, East Asia and Australia are dark navy, meaning near-universal registration. Lighter shades are concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa and parts of South Asia, with medium blue across South Asia. Five countries are outlined and labeled. Somalia has the lowest rate at 3 percent, followed by Zambia at 8 percent, Papua New Guinea at 13 percent, Chad at 26 percent and Pakistan at 42 percent. Ethiopia is also shaded among the palest countries. Much of Central, East and Southern Africa appears in light to medium blue.",data:"birth_registration_choropleth",anchorKey:"5786c78bbbf8",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,legend_title:"Share of children under 5 with birth registration"},{_key:"dd30b6c9c283-8d7250c7b94d",type:"text",text:"However, the gap does not close on a child’s fifth birthday. A child who is not registered often stays unregistered until they are old enough to apply for an adult ID, usually between the ages of 15 and 18. Furthermore it is often very difficult for people whose birth has not been registered to apply for an ID. "},{_key:"dd30b6c9c283-0e77f8688436",type:"text",text:`Globally, approximately 458 million children, who are below the ID eligibility age of their country, are missing birth registration, making them legally invisible for their governments.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_3_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_3_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"4bf7c511a970",type:"factoid",title:"As of 2025",number:"458",units:"million",label:"children (below the age of ID eligibility) are missing birth registration, globally"},{_key:"7e4249a6099b-e5f8059bea8c",type:"text",text:`The effects extend beyond individuals. Incomplete birth registration means governments have an incomplete picture of their populations, making it harder to plan services, direct resources, and identify communities that are being left behind.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 3"><label for='reference_3_5_169125019' aria-hidden="true">3</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_3_5_169125019'/><div class="details">UNICEF. (2024). <a href="https://data.unicef.org/resources/the-right-start-in-life-2024-update/" target="_blank" rel="external">The right start in life: Global levels and trends in birth registration 2024 update</a>. New York, NY: United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF).</div></div><div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 4"><label for='reference_4_2_885055261' aria-hidden="true">4</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_4_2_885055261'/><div class="details">Kusumaputra, I. M., Mahardika, M. C., Hayati, B. N., & Itu, Y. (2025). <a href="https://hal.science/hal-05113370v1/document" target="_blank" rel="external">Exclusion from birth: Structural barriers and social impacts absence of birth certificates for children of urban vulnerable groups</a>. The Journal of Philosophical Economics: Reflections on Economic and Social Issues (in press). HAL Archive. </div></div>`},{_key:"d9458ce31170",type:"subhed",text:"Adults without foundational ID"},{_key:"fcde4677a488-653dabab9f33",type:"text",text:"Most regions have achieved near universal coverage when it comes to adult ID. Coverage is lowest in Sub Saharan Africa, but the region is making fast progress."},{_key:"4b079677afb7-9e0972724f5f",type:"text_emphasis",text:`For adults, proof of legal identity is usually provided through foundational ID systems, such as: national ID, population registries, and civil registration. In some contexts it is provided through functional ID issued for specific purposes, such as a passport, drivers’ license, or voter ID.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_4_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_4_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"89dacd358301-ebb5e7be2017",type:"text",text:"In recent years several Sub-Saharan African and South Asian countries have made rapid progress in expanding the availability of modern forms of identification for the general population. "},{_key:"89dacd358301-2e8c70a3cc32",type:"text",text:'Through initiatives like the <a href="https://id.gov.et/worldbank" target="_blank" rel="external">Fayda ID</a> program in Ethiopia, and the <a href="https://projects.worldbank.org/en/projects-operations/projects-list?os=0&srt=id&order=desc&qterm=P169594" target="_blank" rel="external">West Africa Unique Identification for Regional Integration and Inclusion (WURI) Project</a> in Benin, Burkina Faso, Togo and Niger – both supported by the World Bank – millions of people have obtained a government-recognized digital ID that facilitates access to services.'},{_key:"38a7bf77ac72",type:"scroller",graphic:"with_id_region_trends",title:"Large disparities in adult ID coverage persist across Sub-Saharan Africa",subtitle:"Adults with official ID (%)",source:'<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a>',visSize:"default",visdescription:"This animated line chart has four steps. Each step shows adult ID coverage, in percent, at three points in time: 2017, 2021 and 2025. The first step compares regions. The world average stays nearly flat, at 94 percent in 2017 and 95 percent in both 2021 and 2025. The other regions, shown as gray lines, sit close to the world average near the top of the chart. Sub-Saharan Africa, in orange, sits well below them but rises steadily, from 71 percent in 2017 to 76 percent in 2021 and 81 percent in 2025. The next steps show about 30 individual Sub-Saharan African economies as faint orange lines spread between about 15 and 99 percent. The second step highlights the economies with near-universal coverage above 90 percent in 2025, including Mauritius (about 99 percent), Namibia, Kenya and Burkina Faso. The third step highlights two fast improvers. Malawi rose steeply from about 16 percent in 2017 to about 85 percent in 2021 and about 88 percent in 2025. Togo stayed near 40 percent through 2021, then rose to about 62 percent in 2025. The final step highlights economies that remain below 70 percent in 2025. Uganda declined from about 81 percent in 2017 to about 67 percent. Mozambique is at about 62 percent, the Republic of Congo about 56 percent, Niger about 53 percent and Guinea about 47 percent. Sierra Leone has the lowest coverage, at about 43 percent.",data:"hasid_trends",anchorKey:"38a7bf77ac72",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,other_regions:"Other regions",scenes:[{_key:"c5e74f58589b",type:"scene",id:"regions",text:'In <span data-color-id="SSF" class="SSF">Sub-Saharan Africa</span>, nearly 20 percent of adults lack an official ID, compared with just 5 percent globally.'},{_key:"166eedf2d385",type:"scene",id:"countries_high",text:"Nine economies in the region have achieved near-universal (above 90 percent) adult ID coverage: Botswana, Burkina Faso, Ethiopia, Kenya, Madagascar, Mauritania, Mauritius, Namibia, and Zimbabwe."},{_key:"ecb9a957cfc4",type:"scene",id:"malawi_togo",text:"Between 2017 and 2025, Malawi increased foundational ID coverage for adults by 72 percentage points, from 16 percent to 88 percent. Togo also made significant progress."},{_key:"f1b386996f11",type:"scene",id:"countries_low",text:"But eight other economies in the region are still struggling with adult ID coverage rates below 70 percent. This includes Guinea, Mozambique, Niger, the Republic of Congo, Tanzania, Togo, Uganda, and Sierra Leone, where coverage falls to a global low of 43 percent."},{_key:"fa28f4a8fa4b",type:"scene",id:"eap",text:"Outside Africa, the Lao People’s Democratic Republic is the only economy with adult ID coverage rate below 70 percent. "},{_key:"273ff89a2df2",type:"scene",id:"all_countries",text:"Select an economy name to see its foundational ID ownership rates among adults."}]},{_key:"6442196386aa-894673434060",type:"text",text:"Data are lacking for several lower income economies that are facing ongoing or recent conflict, such as Yemen or Syria, but ID coverage is known to be low."},{_key:"6442196386aa-90da234bc082",type:"text",text:""},{_key:"2540e77cebd7",type:"subhed",text:"Structural barriers to obtaining identification "},{_key:"d5c6bd17f83c-dca404063ee9",type:"text",text:"For millions of people, getting an official ID involves more than completing a registration form. Long distances to registration centers, missing supporting documents, and direct or indirect costs are common barriers that prevent people from obtaining an ID, particularly if they already face social and economic disadvantages."},{_key:"20aec964ce48",type:"vis",graphic:"id_ownership_barriers",title:"Distances, missing documents, and costs are the leading reported barriers to obtaining an ID",subtitle:"",source:`<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a> <div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_262081811_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_262081811_1'/><div class="details">The graph shows the reasons given (multiple selection) for not having a foundational or primary ID by people without official ID surveyed in the eight economies in Sub-Saharan Africa with ID coverage rates below 70 percent: Guinea, Mozambique, Niger, the Republic of Congo, Sierra Leone, Tanzania, Togo, and Uganda. Means are calculated with global weights. Includes respondents aged 15 and over who are eligible for an adult ID.</div></div>`,visSize:"default",visdescription:'A dot plot shows how often adults without an ID give each of six reasons for not having one. Each orange dot is one of eight Sub-Saharan African economies with low ID coverage, and a blue tick marks the average. The horizontal axis runs from 0 to 100 percent of adults without an ID. "Needs to travel too far" is the most common reason, with an average of 45 percent and country values from about 36 to 59 percent. "Missing necessary documents" averages 42 percent, ranging from about 28 to 66 percent. "Too expensive to get an ID" averages 40 percent and has the widest spread, from about 19 to 74 percent. "Has other form of identification" averages 28 percent. "Does not need ID" averages 19 percent. "Uncomfortable sharing personal data" is the least common reason, averaging 17 percent, with all countries below about 30 percent.',data:"id_ownership_barriers",anchorKey:"20aec964ce48",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,too_far:"Needs to travel too far",missing_documents:"Missing necessary documents",too_expensive:"Too expensive to get an ID",other_id:"Has other form of identification",not_needed:"Does not need ID",sharing_data:"Uncomfortable sharing personal data",x_axis_title:"Reporting frequency of reasons for not having an ID (% of adults age 15+ without an ID)",average:"Average"},{_key:"08f0d106773b-a6786444b28f",type:"text",text:"Missing documentation can create a cycle of exclusion. A birth certificate may be required to obtain a foundational ID; yet for those whose births were never registered, there may be no straightforward way to obtain one. Lacking one document can prevent people from obtaining another."},{_key:"67be16fc9bc2-939761e146f0",type:"text",text:`These barriers fall hardest on vulnerable groups: in low-income economies with ID coverage below 90 percent, adults with primary education or less are nearly 12 percentage points less likely to have an ID. People who are out of the workforce, live in rural areas, or have a lower income, and women are also disadvantaged, and when these factors overlap, as they often do, the risk of exclusion is compounded.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_5_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_5_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"6ba89eecd74a",type:"subhed",text:"Limited access to essential services"},{_key:"6d172e1cb1a0-7eb0e8f38f5b",type:"text",text:`Without an official ID, routine activities such as obtaining mobile service, opening a bank account, applying for a job, or receiving public support can become difficult or impossible. In approximately 160 economies, proof of identity is required to register a SIM card for a cell phone or other mobile device.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 6"><label for='reference_6_1_1177065407' aria-hidden="true">6</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_6_1_1177065407'/><div class="details">"GSMA. 2025. “<a href="https://www.gsma.com/solutions-and-impact/connectivity-for-good/public-policy/mobile-policy-handbook/consumer-protection/mandatory-registration-of-prepaid-sims/" target="_blank" rel="external">Mandatory registration of prepaid SIMs</a>” Mobile Policy Handbook. Retrieved March 10, 2026</div></div> In Uganda almost 80 percent of the adults without an ID reported being unable to obtain mobile service, greatly limiting their access to connectivity and an expanding range of digital services, as well as their ability to benefit from the digital economy.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_6_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_6_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"b3b7d2b44ae7",type:"vis",graphic:"no_id_challenges",title:"For adults without ID, getting a SIM card is the most common challenge",subtitle:"",source:`<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a> <div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1930967439_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1930967439_1'/><div class="details">The graph shows the difficulties in accessing various services, benefits, and rights (multiple selection) as a result of not having an official foundational or primary ID, reported by people without official ID in the eight economies in Sub-Saharan Africa with ID coverage rates below 70 percent: Guinea, Mozambique, Niger, the Republic of Congo, Sierra Leone, Tanzania, Togo, and Uganda. Means are calculated with global weights. Includes respondents aged 15 and over who are eligible for an adult ID.</div></div>`,visSize:"default",visdescription:"A dot plot shows how often adults without an ID report difficulties in six areas of life because they have no ID. Each orange dot is one of eight Sub-Saharan African economies with low ID coverage, and a blue tick marks the average. Obtaining a SIM card or mobile service is by far the most common challenge, averaging 53 percent. Country values range from about 17 percent to nearly 80 percent, the highest of any category. Participating in elections averages 35 percent, with one country above 60 percent. Applying for a job and using financial services each average 33 percent. Receiving government financial support averages 28 percent. Receiving medical care is the least reported challenge, averaging 23 percent, with all countries between about 11 and 33 percent.",data:"no_id_challenges",anchorKey:"b3b7d2b44ae7",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,sim:"Obtaining a SIM card/mobile service",elections:"Participating in elections",job:"Applying for a job",financial:"Using financial services",government_support:"Receiving government financial support",medical_care:"Receiving medical care",x_axis_title:"Reporting frequency of challenges tied to not owning an ID (% of adults age 15+)",average:"Average"},{_key:"3dd50ccda24c-19ded9d9b575",type:"text",text:"The lack of an official ID can also greatly compromise other aspects of a person’s role in society. In Mozambique, more than 40 percent of adults without official ID report difficulty participating in elections, illustrating how gaps in identification can also restrict civic participation."},{_key:"7bc0ef64e912",type:"subhed",text:"Digital identification"},{_key:"ffb5cd5833fa-0fe092c4ba12",type:"text",text:`Official IDs are often required for accessing social assistance, healthcare, financial services, formal employment, and other services.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 2"><label for='reference_2_2_1129214132' aria-hidden="true">2</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_2_2_1129214132'/><div class="details">Clark, J.M., Metz, A.Z., & Casher, C.S. (2022). <a href="http://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099705012232226786" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Database 2021: Global ID Coverage Estimates.</a> Washington, DC: World Bank.</div></div> `},{_key:"ffb5cd5833fa-0dda06f53866",type:"text",text:`Digital IDs extend the benefits of ID to the digital world by enabling people to prove and authenticate their identity anytime, anywhere. They allow people to prove who they are remotely and securely, and then reuse their trusted digital identity across different public and private services, without having to verify their identity each time. As more transactions move online, economies are increasingly developing digital ID ecosystems as part of their digital public infrastructure.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 7"><label for='reference_7_1_443241524' aria-hidden="true">7</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_7_1_443241524'/><div class="details">World Bank. 2022. <a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/pt/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099745012232218303" target="_blank" rel="external">Federated Ecosystems for Digital ID: Current Approaches and Lessons.</a> Washington, DC: World Bank Group.</div></div>`},{_key:"59230d5da7b2-e0c2cae18064",type:"text",text:`Access to digital identification remains highly unequal: an estimated 2.8 billion people live in economies (mostly low- and lower-middle-income) without a digital ID system that is capable of secure remote authentication.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_7_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_7_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"b6583151cfb5-97820bf0e898",type:"text",text:`Digital identification can take different forms, and these build on each other. At the most basic level, identity records are stored electronically rather than on paper. At the next level, identity information can be checked digitally during in-person transactions. At the most advanced level, people can securely prove who they are remotely, to access services and transact online.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 8"><label for='reference_8_1_534714553' aria-hidden="true">8</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_8_1_534714553'/><div class="details">Metz, A., Casher, C., and Clark, J. (2024). <a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099020824141510923" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset Volume 2: Digital Identification Progress and Gaps</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"9f1ae372c4d4",type:"vis",graphic:"id_illustration",title:"Types of ID Credentials",subtitle:"",source:"Adapted from ID4D Global Dataset 2021: Volume 3 - Trends in Identification for Development",visSize:"small",visdescription:"A diagram shows identity credentials in three groups, set side by side as colored columns that overlap where categories are shared. On the left, in yellow, are physical documents, which present identity information non-digitally. These are documents with no machine readability. In the middle, in light blue, are digital credentials, which attest to a person's identity in a digital environment. These are mobile ID, digital certificate, user name and ID number. The digital credentials band extends to cover two overlapping categories. Digitized cards and documents, in green, overlap physical documents. Biometric measures, in bright blue, overlap the additional factors group. On the right, in dark blue, are additional factors, which confirm that a person is associated with a digital credential. These are PIN, password, one-time password (OTP), token and security question. The layout shows how credentials range from purely physical to fully digital, with biometrics serving both as a credential and as an authentication factor.",anchorKey:"9f1ae372c4d4",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,physical_title:"Physical Documents",physical_caption:"Present identity information non-digitally",digital_title:"Digital Credentials",digital_caption:"Attest to a person's identity in a digital environment (i.e., used for digital authentication)",factors_title:"Additional Factors",factors_caption:"Confirm a person is associated with a digital credential",label_no_machine_readability:"Documents with no machine readability",label_digitized:"Digitized Cards & Documents",label_mobile_id:"Mobile ID",label_digital_certificate:"Digital Certificate",label_user_name:"User Name",label_id_number:"ID Number",label_pin:"PIN",label_password:"Password",label_otp:"OTP",label_token:"Token",label_question:"Question",label_biometric:"Biometric Measure"},{_key:"db11c173a7e8-7ea03198c63f",type:"text",text:"The World Bank counts an economy as having a digital ID system when it is at the most advanced level. For this, it needs to pass three tests. First, the system is securely linked to the real person who holds it. Second, it is widely used by the general public rather than only in pilots. And third, it can be used across multiple online services. By this measure, only 89 economies had digital ID systems in 2024."},{_key:"d975155f0507",type:"vis",graphic:"economies_with_eid",title:"Digital IDs are still not available in many economies ",subtitle:"",source:`<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a> <div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_1659988272_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_1659988272_1'/><div class="details">The graph uses a binary indicator of whether an economy has an operational digital ID system, based on the methodology outlined in Metz et al. (2024); The data are based on digital ID status as recorded by the World Bank in 2024.</div></div>`,visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"A unit chart uses one small square per economy to compare economies with and without a digital ID system, grouped by income level. Two economies are labeled as examples in each group. Among low-income economies, only 1 of 23 has a digital ID (Uganda), while 22 do not (Rwanda is an example). Among lower-middle-income economies, 7 of 47 have one (Morocco) and 40 do not (Bangladesh). Among upper-middle-income economies, the split is close: 27 of 58 have one (Ukraine) and 31 do not (Mexico). Among high-income economies, 54 of 66 have one (Chile) and 12 do not (Nauru). Overall, 89 economies have a digital ID system. Availability rises sharply with income, and almost all low-income economies lack one.",data:"economies_with_eid",anchorKey:"d975155f0507",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,with_eid:"Economies with Digital ID",without_eid:"Economies without Digital ID",without_eid_mobile:"Without Digital ID",keyFact:"2.8 billion",keyFactLabel:"people live in economies without access to digital IDs. "},{_key:"d6b562ea4324-3f0fd8a0885f",type:"text",text:`Availability alone is not enough. Even where access to digital ID systems exists, many people remain unenrolled, and use often lags ownership.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_8_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_8_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"d6b562ea4324-e0c2cae18064",type:"text",text:""},{_key:"537107715a83",type:"scroller",graphic:"eid_ownership_use_scatter",title:"Millions hold a digital ID they have never used",subtitle:"Share of the population that own vs. have used a digital ID, by economy. The longer the line, the bigger the gap between owning and using.",source:'<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a>',visSize:"default",visdescription:"This animated scatterplot has multiple steps. Each dot is an economy, colored by income group: dark teal for high income, green for upper-middle income and pink for lower-middle income. The vertical axis shows the share of the population that owns a digital ID, and the horizontal axis shows the share that has used one, both from 0 to 100 percent. A diagonal line marks where everyone who owns a digital ID also uses it. All dots sit above the line, meaning ownership exceeds use everywhere. A horizontal gray line connects each dot to the diagonal, and its length shows the gap between owning and using. The dots follow a clear upward trend: economies with higher ownership also tend to have higher use. High-income economies cluster toward the upper right, while lower-middle-income economies sit at the lower left. The first step highlights Japan, which has the widest gap: about 79 percent own a digital ID but only about 28 percent have used one. Other large gaps include a lower-middle-income economy at about 69 percent ownership and 20 percent use, and an upper-middle-income economy at about 50 percent ownership and 5 percent use. The next step shows all gaps without a highlight. The last step removes the gap lines and highlights the two ends of the range. Sweden is in the top right corner, with about 97 percent ownership and about 95 percent use. Peru is near the bottom left, with about 14 percent ownership and about 4 percent use.",data:"eid_ownership_use",anchorKey:"537107715a83",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,own:"Share of population that owns a digital ID",use:"Share of population that has used a digital ID",equality:"Line where everyone who has a digital ID also uses it",gallery_thumbnail:"id4d_digital_id_use_scatter",scenes:[{_key:"85e8edbc69d4",type:"scene",id:"intro",text:"As this scatterplot shows, in general, the more people in a country have a digital ID, the larger the share of the population that has used one."},{_key:"90935b6c80d8",type:"scene",id:"gaps",text:"The gap between ownership and use (shown in the chart by the gray lines) varies substantially across economies."},{_key:"c338c4c42373",type:"scene",id:"japan",text:"Japan has the widest gap: almost 80 percent of people own a digital ID, but only 28 percent have used one."},{_key:"bb14f3c45752",type:"scene",id:"nordics",text:"In contrast, in some European countries — including Sweden, Denmark, Iceland and Norway — more than 90 percent of people own and have used a digital ID."}]},{_key:"b5a1f2e5f038-0c419f7c1cca",type:"text",text:`Meaningful use depends on many things. This includes whether digital IDs are accepted across public and private services, whether people have the technology and skills needed to use them, how user-friendly the technology is, and whether legal and governance frameworks protect users’ rights. Above all, people need to see a clear benefit in using their credentials.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_9_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_9_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"58faf33f40fc",type:"vis",graphic:"government_service_access",title:"People with a digital ID use online government services much more often",subtitle:"",source:`<a href="https://id4d.worldbank.org/global-dataset" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D-Global Findex Database 2025</a> <div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_174304199_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_174304199_1'/><div class="details">The sample comprises low- and middle-income economies with sufficient sample size (digital ID ownership of 4 percent or higher), selected by highest online government service access rates and ranked by adult population. Includes respondents aged 15 and over who are eligible for an adult ID.</div></div>`,visSize:"default",visdescription:"A dumbbell chart compares ten economies on the share of people who accessed government services online in the past three months. For each economy, an orange dot shows people without a digital ID and a blue dot shows people with one, connected by a line. In every economy, people with a digital ID use online government services more. Thailand has the highest rates: 48 percent without and 66 percent with. Türkiye goes from 26 to 64 percent, Jordan from 27 to 60 percent, and Brazil from 11 to 57 percent, the largest gap at 46 percentage points. Mongolia goes from 11 to 45 percent, Indonesia from 28 to 45 percent, Argentina from 21 to 43 percent, Paraguay from 16 to 41 percent, Peru from 20 to 40 percent, and the Kyrgyz Republic from 17 to 36 percent. In most of these economies, people with a digital ID are two to three times as likely, or more, to use online government services.",data:"government_service_access",anchorKey:"58faf33f40fc",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,digital_id:"Has digital ID",no_digital_id:"No digital ID",x_axis_units:"%",x_axis_title:"Share of people who accessed government services online in the last three months",keyFact:"2–3 times",keyFactLabel:"higher — the rate at which adults with digital IDs access online government services, compared with those without."},{_key:"e86f00e4fd27",type:"subhed",text:"Ethiopia: from fragmented paper records to a national digital ID"},{_key:"c7e79254ef5e-06da83427b15",type:"text",text:`Until recently, Ethiopia lacked a national foundational ID system with digital verification. In practice, people relied on locally issued paper IDs, which varied widely and were difficult for service providers to authenticate. In 2017, more than 35 percent of adults lacked even this form of identification, with substantially wider gaps among women.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 9"><label for='reference_9_1_1340157368' aria-hidden="true">9</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_9_1_1340157368'/><div class="details">World Bank. 2023. <a href="https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099112223132535848/pdf/BOSIB0efb09b920d90858a0135df22da7d1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Ethiopia Digital ID for Inclusion and Services Project: Project Appraisal Document</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank.</div></div>`},{_key:"c7e79254ef5e-4ad7a4fd89cd",type:"text",text:`Ethiopia is now rolling out Fayda ID, a unified national digital ID designed to give residents secure, government-recognized proof of identity. The World Bank-supported “Digital ID for Inclusion and Services Project” is helping expand registration, strengthen data-protection institutions, and integrate the  Fayda ID into public and private services.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 10"><label for='reference_10_1_671808518' aria-hidden="true">10</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_10_1_671808518'/><div class="details">World Bank. 2026. <a href="https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099051826153515642/pdf/P179040-74a164c1-0bb2-49b6-8b5f-2da36395da02.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Ethiopia Digital ID for Inclusion and Services Project (P179040): Implementation Support Review, March 23–30, 2026: Aide Memoire</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank.</div></div> `},{_key:"724854095309-4ad7a4fd89cd",type:"text_emphasis",text:`By October 2026, more than 54 million people in Ethiopia (or approximately 40 percent of the total population) had been registered in the Fayda digital ID program.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 11"><label for='reference_11_1_1725179633' aria-hidden="true">11</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_11_1_1725179633'/><div class="details">National ID Program (NIDP), Ethiopia. 2026. <a href="https://www.id.gov.et/" target="_blank" rel="external">Fayda: Ethiopia’s Digital ID</a>.</div></div>`},{_key:"da886c3d8adb-4b53a0594473",type:"text",text:`Early process testing illustrates the potential benefits. In controlled comparisons, Fayda-enabled verification reduced the estimated time and cost of opening a bank account and obtaining a SIM card. These findings are projections rather than measured system-wide outcomes, but they show how trusted digital verification could make routine service provision faster and more accessible.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 12"><label for='reference_12_1_2011425985' aria-hidden="true">12</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_12_1_2011425985'/><div class="details">Ethiopian National ID Program. n.d. <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tXa3C20Y7jWl3WQg-jKqpo8sBqDfZO_2/preview" target="_blank" rel="external">Fayda Integration: Evidence of Faster, Smarter Service Delivery</a>. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia: Ethiopian National ID Program.</div></div>`},{_key:"1c46dc57f881",type:"vis",graphic:"service_delivery_improvement",title:"The introduction of digital IDs in Ethiopia is projected to improve service delivery",subtitle:"Projected changes, based on observed processes and Fayda testing",source:`<a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tXa3C20Y7jWl3WQg-jKqpo8sBqDfZO_2/preview" target="_blank" rel="external">Ethiopian National ID Program</a> <div class="summary"><div class="bg" aria-label="footnote"><label class="footnote" aria-hidden="true" for='footnote_344012572_1'>i</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read footnote" id='footnote_344012572_1'/><div class="details">The Ethiopian National ID Program and World Bank ID4D measured the pre-Fayda ("as-is") process through 228 structured observations at 16 service sites across Addis Ababa, Sheger City, and Afar, then lab-tested the Fayda-enabled ("to-be") equivalent and projected the savings.</div></div>`,visSize:"default",visdescription:"Two rows of paired bar charts show projected changes from Ethiopia's Fayda digital ID for two services: opening a bank account and SIM card issuance. Each service has three panels: service time in minutes, provider cost in dollars and client cost in dollars. In each panel, a dark navy bar shows the value before Fayda and a shorter blue bar shows the projected value after, with the saved portion in light gray. For opening a bank account, service time falls from 30 minutes to about 9 minutes (a 70 percent reduction). Provider cost falls from just over 1 dollar to about 0.76 dollars (27 percent), and client cost falls from about 0.70 dollars to about 0.27 dollars (61 percent). For SIM card issuance, service time falls from 20 minutes to about 9 minutes (55 percent). Provider cost falls from about 0.64 dollars to about 0.32 dollars (50 percent), and client cost falls slightly, from about 0.31 dollars to about 0.27 dollars (13 percent). The projections are based on 228 observations at 16 service sites and lab testing of the Fayda-enabled process.",data:"service_delivery_improvement",anchorKey:"1c46dc57f881",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,bank_account_opening:"Opening a bank account",attorney_registration:"Registration of power of attorney",sim_card_issuance:"SIM card issuance",service_time:"Service time",provider_cost:"Provider cost",client_cost:"Client cost",before_label:"Before",after_label:"After",minutes:"Minutes",dollars:"Dollars",reduction:"reduction"},{_key:"cbea121766b4",type:"subhed",text:"How ID4D is helping countries close the identification gap"},{_key:"b89bbef78bd1-4e3aa75db816",type:"text",text:"The World Bank’s Identification for Development (ID4D) Initiative helps countries build inclusive, trusted, and effective identification systems so that everyone can prove who they are and access services, rights, and economic opportunities. ID4D currently works with more than 80 countries to make identification more accessible, secure, and useful."},{_key:"f7ccbd754f4e-4e3aa75db816",type:"text",text:`ID4D supports everything from birth registration and national ID systems to digital identity tools for online and in-person services. It also helps countries strengthen the laws, safeguards, and technology behind these systems and shares research and lessons on what does and does not work.<div class="summary reference wide"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 13"><label for='reference_13_1_123440517' aria-hidden="true">13</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_13_1_123440517'/><div class="details">World Bank. 2025. <a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099537409182599392" target="_blank" rel="external">Annual Report 2024–25: 10 Years of Impact. Putting People at the Center of Digital Transformation</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank Group.</div></div>`},{_key:"e25789d301ae",type:"vis",graphic:"id4d_countries",title:"Countries with World Bank — ID4D engagement",subtitle:"",source:'<a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099537409182599392" target="_blank" rel="external">World Bank (2025)</a>',visSize:"fullScreen",visdescription:"A world map shows, in bright blue, the countries where the World Bank's Identification for Development (ID4D) initiative is active. All other countries are light gray. Engagement covers more than 80 countries and is concentrated in Sub-Saharan Africa. There, most countries across West, Central and East Africa are highlighted, along with Ethiopia, Somalia, Angola, Mozambique and Madagascar. In other regions, highlighted countries include Morocco, Tunisia and Jordan; Ukraine, parts of the Western Balkans and the South Caucasus; Kazakhstan and other Central Asian countries; Mongolia; Pakistan, India, Bangladesh, Nepal and Sri Lanka; Thailand, Cambodia, Lao PDR, Indonesia and the Philippines; and Brazil, Peru, Honduras and Haiti. North America, most of Europe, Russia, China, Australia, most of the Gulf, and much of southern Africa are not highlighted.",data:"id4d_countries",anchorKey:"e25789d301ae",disableDataDownload:!1,disableScreenshot:!1,diagnostic:"Diagnostic",technical_assistance:"Technical assistance",research_mel:"Research",active:"Financing for Identification and Civil Registration (Active)"},{_key:"5f7a691da85e-bb538418b599",type:"text",text:"Closing the identification gap requires going beyond issuing credentials to ensuring that identity systems are inclusive, trusted, and useful. Priorities include: strengthening civil registration from birth; removing cost, distance, and documentation requirement barriers; designing systems for underserved populations; and ensuring ID systems enable access to essential services and economic opportunities. "},{_key:"5f7a691da85e-a13e44c58b97",type:"text",text:`Digital ID systems must also be accessible to people with limited access to technology or digital skills, and widely accepted across public and private services. Ultimately, success should be measured by whether everyone can reliably prove their identity, in person or online, in ways that allow them to access services, expand opportunities, and protect their rights.<div class="summary reference"><div class="bg" aria-label="reference 1"><label for='reference_1_10_696312901' aria-hidden="true">1</label></div><input type="checkbox" role="switch" aria-label="read reference" id='reference_1_10_696312901'/><div class="details">Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.</div></div>`}],t=[{text:'Ansar, S.; Clark, J.M.; Wolff, J.M.. (2026). <a href="https://openknowledge.worldbank.org/entities/publication/3c36d203-5ed0-419c-84df-3cc850f242e4" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset 2025: Trends in Identification for Development</a>. © World Bank.',index:0},{text:'Clark, J.M., Metz, A.Z., & Casher, C.S. (2022). <a href="http://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099705012232226786" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Database 2021: Global ID Coverage Estimates.</a> Washington, DC: World Bank.',index:1},{text:`UNICEF. (2024). <a href="https://data.unicef.org/resources/the-right-start-in-life-2024-update/" target="_blank" rel="external">The right start in life: Global levels and trends in birth registration 2024 update</a>. New York, NY: United Nations Children's Fund (UNICEF).`,index:2},{text:'Kusumaputra, I. M., Mahardika, M. C., Hayati, B. N., & Itu, Y. (2025). <a href="https://hal.science/hal-05113370v1/document" target="_blank" rel="external">Exclusion from birth: Structural barriers and social impacts absence of birth certificates for children of urban vulnerable groups</a>. The Journal of Philosophical Economics: Reflections on Economic and Social Issues (in press). HAL Archive. ',index:3},{text:'European Network on Statelessness (ENS). (2024). <a href="https://www.statelessness.eu/sites/default/files/2024-11/ENS_Birth_Registration_briefing_Nov2024_FINALDESIGN_1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Birth registration and the prevention of statelessness in Europe: Identifying good practices and remaining barriers (Thematic briefing, updated November 2024)</a>. European Network on Statelessness.',index:4},{text:'"GSMA. 2025. “<a href="https://www.gsma.com/solutions-and-impact/connectivity-for-good/public-policy/mobile-policy-handbook/consumer-protection/mandatory-registration-of-prepaid-sims/" target="_blank" rel="external">Mandatory registration of prepaid SIMs</a>” Mobile Policy Handbook. Retrieved March 10, 2026',index:5},{text:'World Bank. 2022. <a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/pt/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099745012232218303" target="_blank" rel="external">Federated Ecosystems for Digital ID: Current Approaches and Lessons.</a> Washington, DC: World Bank Group.',index:6},{text:'Metz, A., Casher, C., and Clark, J. (2024). <a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099020824141510923" target="_blank" rel="external">ID4D Global Dataset Volume 2: Digital Identification Progress and Gaps</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank.',index:7},{text:'World Bank. 2023. <a href="https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099112223132535848/pdf/BOSIB0efb09b920d90858a0135df22da7d1.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Ethiopia Digital ID for Inclusion and Services Project: Project Appraisal Document</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank.',index:8},{text:'World Bank. 2026. <a href="https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099051826153515642/pdf/P179040-74a164c1-0bb2-49b6-8b5f-2da36395da02.pdf" target="_blank" rel="external">Ethiopia Digital ID for Inclusion and Services Project (P179040): Implementation Support Review, March 23–30, 2026: Aide Memoire</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank.',index:9},{text:'National ID Program (NIDP), Ethiopia. 2026. <a href="https://www.id.gov.et/" target="_blank" rel="external">Fayda: Ethiopia’s Digital ID</a>.',index:10},{text:'Ethiopian National ID Program. n.d. <a href="https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tXa3C20Y7jWl3WQg-jKqpo8sBqDfZO_2/preview" target="_blank" rel="external">Fayda Integration: Evidence of Faster, Smarter Service Delivery</a>. Addis Ababa, Ethiopia: Ethiopian National ID Program.',index:11},{text:'World Bank. 2025. <a href="https://documents.worldbank.org/en/publication/documents-reports/documentdetail/099537409182599392" target="_blank" rel="external">Annual Report 2024–25: 10 Years of Impact. Putting People at the Center of Digital Transformation</a>. Washington, DC: World Bank Group.',index:12}],i={config:e,content:a,references:t};export{e as config,a as content,i as default,t as references};
